        "title": "Warranty: What It Covers and What It Doesn't",
        "points": [
          {
            "label": "Manufacturing vs. Wear & Tear",
            "text": "Warranty covers defects in the product itself (broken component, faulty assembly), not damage from misuse. Dropping a phone and cracking the screen = misuse (not covered). Phone randomly shutting down = manufacturing defect (covered)."
          },
          {
            "label": "Implied Warranty",
            "text": "Indian law assumes all products should be usable for a 'reasonable period' even without explicit warranty. A fridge should last 5+ years; a phone 3+ years. If it breaks before then, it's assumed defective (burden on company to prove misuse)."
          },
          {
            "label": "Warranty Period Misconceptions",
            "text": "'1-year warranty' doesn't mean the product fails after 1 year. It means (1) if defects appear within 1 year, company fixes free, (2) after 1 year, repairs cost money. But implied warranty extends beyond—if a fridge dies after 4 years, company must prove the customer abused it."
          },
          {
            "label": "Voiding Warranty",
            "text": "Companies can void warranty only if: (1) customer physically damaged the product, (2) customer opened the product (violates 'sealed' warranty), or (3) customer used it for unintended purpose. Hidden reasons to void warranty are illegal."
          }
        ],
