          "solution": "Epigenetics: DNA methylation, histone modifications mark genes 'on' or 'off' without changing sequence. Early development: environmental cues (nutrition, stress) alter methylation patterns. If Twin A is well-fed, growth genes demethylate (activate); if Twin B is undernourished, growth genes methylate (silence). These marks can be heritable through cell divisions. Some are even transgenerational (grandparent's diet affects grandchild's metabolism via epigenetic inheritance). This explains phenotypic variation without genotypic change."
