        "storyContext": "Priya dissects a pig heart and sees the major vessels. Dr. Kapoor explains: \"Arteries are thick and muscular—they carry blood under pressure. Veins are thin—blood flows slowly. Capillaries are where the real work happens.\"",
        "points": [
          {
            "label": "Arteries",
            "text": "Thick muscular walls; narrow lumen. Carry oxygenated blood from heart (systemic arteries) or deoxygenated to lungs (pulmonary artery). Pressure: 120/80 mmHg (systolic/diastolic)."
          },
          {
            "label": "Capillaries",
            "text": "Ultrathin walls (one cell layer). Diameter ~7 μm (just wide enough for one RBC). Slow blood flow allows gas/nutrient exchange. Intercellular fluid (lymph) leaks out, re-enters mostly via venular capillaries."
          },
          {
            "label": "Veins",
            "text": "Thin, distensible walls; large lumen. Carry blood slowly back to heart. Contain 60% of total blood volume at any time. Pressure: ~2–8 mmHg. Valves prevent backflow."
          }
        ],
        "inSimpleWords": "Arteries are strong tubes that carry blood away from the heart under high pressure. Capillaries are hair-thin and where gas/nutrient exchange happens. Veins are relaxed tubes that return blood to the heart.",
