        "storyContext": "Dr. Kapoor measures Rohan's blood pressure: 110/70 mmHg. \"Normal for your age. The first number is systolic—when the ventricle contracts. The second is diastolic—when it relaxes.\"",
        "points": [
          {
            "label": "Systolic Pressure (120 mmHg)",
            "text": "Maximum pressure when left ventricle contracts and ejects blood into the aorta."
          },
          {
            "label": "Diastolic Pressure (80 mmHg)",
            "text": "Minimum pressure when ventricle is relaxed and refilling from the atrium."
          },
          {
            "label": "Regulation",
            "text": "Baroreceptors in carotid sinus and aortic arch sense pressure. If pressure drops, sympathetic nervous system increases heart rate and vessel constriction (raise pressure). If too high, parasympathetic system decreases heart rate and dilates vessels (lower pressure)."
          },
          {
            "label": "Hypertension Risk",
            "text": "Chronic elevation (>140/90 mmHg) damages vessel walls, increasing stroke and heart disease risk."
          }
        ],
        "inSimpleWords": "Blood pressure is the force blood exerts on vessel walls. Systolic is the peak (when the heart contracts), diastolic is the trough (when it relaxes).",
