        "storyContext": "Rohan learns that when blood vessels are damaged, platelets and clotting factors form a plug. But in hemophilia, one clotting factor is missing.",
        "example": {
          "problem": "A hemophiliac (Factor VIII deficiency) gets a nosebleed. Normal clotting cascade: Tissue damage → extrinsic pathway (Factor VII) and intrinsic pathway (Factor VIII, IX, etc.) → common pathway (Factors X, V) → thrombin production → fibrin clot forms. Without Factor VIII, the intrinsic pathway is blocked. Why does the clot form slowly (or not at all)? What treatment is used?",
          "solution": "Factor VIII deficiency removes a critical link in the intrinsic pathway. Thrombin production is dramatically reduced; fibrin formation is delayed. Severe hemophiliacs may have clotting times of 30+ min (vs. normal 8–12 min). Treatment: Factor VIII replacement (from donated plasma or recombinant), or desmopressin (raises endogenous Factor VIII levels). Tranexamic acid (antifibrinolytic) can also help by preventing clot breakdown."
        },
