        "title": "Challenge: Atherosclerosis and Plaque Formation",
        "storyContext": "Dr. Kapoor shows Rohan an angiogram of a diseased artery. \"Years of high cholesterol, smoking, and stress narrow the lumen. Blood clots can get stuck, triggering a heart attack.\"",
        "example": {
          "problem": "A 55-year-old man has a 70% stenosis (narrowing) in his left anterior descending (LAD) coronary artery. Flow rate through arteries follows: Flow = (Pressure gradient) / Resistance. Resistance ∝ 1/radius⁴ (Poiseuille's law). If the artery's radius shrinks from 2 mm to 0.6 mm due to plaque, how does resistance change? What happens to blood flow?",
          "solution": "Normal: R ∝ 1/(2⁴) = 1/16. Stenosed: R ∝ 1/(0.6⁴) = 1/0.13 ≈ 7.7. Resistance increases ~120-fold. With fixed MAP, Flow drops proportionally. At 70% stenosis, the narrowed vessel can only deliver 30% of normal flow. During exercise (higher metabolic demand), the heart muscle gets insufficient oxygen → angina (chest pain). Without intervention (angioplasty, stent, bypass), a blood clot can lodge in the stenosis → complete blockage → myocardial infarction (MI)."
        },
