        "kind": "concept",
        "title": "Elastic vs. Plastic Deformation",
        "storyContext": "Priya explains the two regions on a stress-strain curve: 'Below the yield point, the material springs back (elastic). Beyond it, the material stays deformed permanently (plastic).'",
        "points": [
          {
            "label": "Elastic region",
            "text": "Below yield stress: material returns to original shape when load is removed. Follows Hooke's Law: Stress ∝ Strain."
          },
          {
            "label": "Plastic region",
            "text": "Above yield stress: material stays permanently deformed even after load is removed. Occurs before material breaks."
          },
          {
            "label": "Ductility",
            "text": "Ductile materials (steel) show large plastic region before breaking. Brittle materials (cast iron, glass) show minimal plastic region."
          },
          {
            "label": "Engineer's advantage",
            "text": "Ductile materials give warning (visible deformation) before failure. Brittle materials fail suddenly with no warning."
          }
        ],
        "inSimpleWords": "Steel stretches visibly before breaking—engineers see trouble coming. Cast iron snaps suddenly—dangerous in structures.",
        "emoji": "📈",
