        "storyContext": "Dr. Nair maps three scales: 'Genetic diversity = variation within a species (Bengal tigers have low genetic diversity because only ~50 breeding individuals remain; 400 years ago, maybe 10,000, high diversity). Species diversity = number of species in an area. Ecosystem diversity = variety of habitats: forests, grasslands, wetlands, deserts.'",
        "points": [
          {
            "label": "Genetic Diversity",
            "text": "Variation in alleles within a population. Measured by heterozygosity (H = 1 − Σpi²) or nucleotide polymorphism. High H → population resilient to disease, parasites, environmental change. Low H → inbreeding depression, reduced fitness."
          },
          {
            "label": "Species Diversity",
            "text": "Two components: (1) Species richness (S = number of species). (2) Species evenness (J = Σ(pi × ln(pi)) / ln(S), where pi = proportion of species i). Shannon index (H' = −Σ(pi × ln(pi))) combines both; higher = more diverse."
          },
          {
            "label": "Ecosystem Diversity",
            "text": "Number of distinct habitats/biomes. Example: Western Ghats contain montane forests (3,000+ species), grasslands (200+ species), wetlands (migrant birds, fish). Loss of one ecosystem = extinction risk for specialists."
          }
        ],
