        "title": "Henry's Law: Gases in Solutions",
        "storyContext": "Arjun mentions: 'Solutions can contain dissolved gases too. The amount of gas dissolved depends on pressure and temperature.' This explains why carbonated drinks fizz when opened.",
        "points": [
          {
            "label": "Henry's Law",
            "text": "Concentration of dissolved gas is proportional to its partial pressure. C = kP (where k is Henry's constant)."
          },
          {
            "label": "Effect of Pressure",
            "text": "Higher pressure on gas = more gas dissolves (why sealed soda stays fizzy)."
          },
          {
            "label": "Effect of Temperature",
            "text": "Higher temperature = less gas dissolved (warm soda loses CO₂ faster)."
          }
        ],
        "example": {
          "problem": "At 1 atm pressure and 25°C, 0.034 M CO₂ dissolves in water. At 2 atm and 25°C, what is the concentration?",
          "solution": "C₁ = kP₁. 0.034 = k(1). k = 0.034. C₂ = 0.034 × 2 = 0.068 M. Double the pressure, double the dissolved CO₂."
        },
        "inSimpleWords": "Gases dissolve more when pressure increases and temperature decreases.",
        "emoji": "🫧"
